this is a production of cornell  university  everyone's really happy to be here um  i'm going to give you a pretty quick  presentation about nuts that you can  grow in the northeast  and in general there are about 28  species of edible nuts in the northeast  and all of them are edible or at least  have edible parts some require more  processing than others  but that's just to say that there's a  huge diversity of food that falls to the  ground every fall  and yeah they're all  all enjoyable  but today for this purposes of this  presentation i'm going to focus on two  in particular  chestnuts and hazelnuts i focus on these  because these are the most um  commercially applicable  although you can grow all the other ones  if you're interested in wildlife or  habitat restoration or if you're just  feeling kind of nutty  so don't let my focus on these two  dissuade you from your other interests  starting off with chestnuts  the most important thing about growing  chestnuts is that you're going to want  to choose  a chestnut plant material that is known  to be resistant to the chestnut blight  chestnut blade i'll talk about a little  bit later is the most serious pathogen  threat to chestnuts and so when you're  looking at  um  procuring chestnut plate material you  want to look for chinese chestnuts  chestnuts that's castaneda mulissima or  japanese chestnuts castamia cranata also  their hybrids  um as well as either of those hybrids  along with  the european chestnut a casino sativa  um  chestnuts love really well-drained soils  it's very important to keep  their roots not waterlogged and they  prefer acidic soils about 5.5 to 6 and a  half for ph  in addition to good water drainage they  really also prefer really good frost  drainage while they can tolerate  temperatures of up to negative 20  degrees fahrenheit just want to  be careful with them and so planting  them on slopes achieves both of these  goals in one  they're also very drought tolerant after  establishment